module

public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Divisors.RegularImmersion.RelativeFlatness
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Intersection.ProperIntersections.DiagonalRegularImmersion
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.MoreMorphisms.RegularImmersion.RegularOpenIndependence
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.RegularIdealSheaf.ImmersionStalk

/-!
# The ideal of the diagonal at a point is generated by a regular sequence

Let `X` be a nonsingular variety over a field and let `ξ` be a point of `X`. The diagonal
`Δ : X ⟶ X ×ₖ X` is a regular immersion, so at the point `Δ(ξ)` the kernel of the map of local
rings `𝒪_{X ×ₖ X, Δ(ξ)} ⟶ 𝒪_{X, ξ}` is generated by a regular sequence. This is the input the
reduction to the diagonal needs on the product side: it is what lets the multiplicity formula
for a regular sequence be applied there, where it cannot be applied to the original pair of
subvarieties.

The regularity of the diagonal is Stacks tag 0B0Q,
`Stacks.Intersection.isRegularImmersion_diagonal`. Reading it as a statement about one local
ring is two steps: a regular immersion is a condition on the defining ideal sheaf of the
canonical closed factor, which for a closed immersion may be read on the target itself
(`Stacks.Intersection.Diagonal.isRegularIdealSheaf_of_isRegularImmersion`), and a regular ideal
sheaf has, at a point of the support of its quotient, a stalk generated by a regular sequence
of the local ring
(`Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.RegularIdealSheaf.exists_isRegular_generating_ker_stalkMap`).

## Main results

* `Stacks.Intersection.Diagonal.isRegularIdealSheaf_of_isRegularImmersion` — for a closed
  immersion, regularity as an immersion is regularity of the defining ideal sheaf on the target.
  This is the converse of `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.isRegularImmersion_of_isRegularIdealSheaf`.
* `Stacks.Intersection.Diagonal.exists_isRegular_generating_ker_stalkMap_of_isRegularImmersion`
  — for a regular closed immersion the kernel of every induced map of local rings is generated
  by a regular sequence.
* `Stacks.Intersection.Diagonal.exists_isRegular_generating_ker_stalkMap_diagonal` — the
  diagonal of a nonsingular variety: the kernel of `𝒪_{X ×ₖ X, Δ(ξ)} ⟶ 𝒪_{X, ξ}` is generated
  by a regular sequence of `𝒪_{X ×ₖ X, Δ(ξ)}`.

## Implementation notes

*The perfect-field hypothesis of Stacks tag 0B0Q.*
`Stacks.Intersection.isRegularImmersion_diagonal` carries `[PerfectField k]`, because it deduces
regularity of the diagonal from smoothness of `X` and of `X ×ₖ X` over `k`, and over a perfect
field regularity of a variety is equivalent to smoothness of its structure morphism. That
hypothesis is not an obstruction here: the chapter on intersection theory fixes an algebraically
closed ground field, and an algebraically closed field is perfect, so the hypothesis is discharged
by the chapter's own convention. It is carried explicitly in the statements below rather than
silently assumed.

*The length of the sequence.* The regular sequence is produced as a `List`, so its length is
whatever the local generators of the regular ideal sheaf happen to have; the identification of
that length with `dim X` — the codimension of the diagonal, recorded in
`Stacks.Intersection.isFiniteLocallyFreeOfRank_conormalSheaf_diagonal` as the rank of the
conormal sheaf and in `Stacks.Intersection.codimension_diagonal_eq_topologicalKrullDim` as a
topological codimension at the generic point — is not proved here, and no statement below
asserts it.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Lemma 43.13.3 (Tag 0B0Q)](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0B0Q)
* [Stacks Project, Definition 31.21.4 (Tag 063J)](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/063J)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0B0U](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0B0U)
-/

@[expose] public section

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits TopologicalSpace
open AlgebraicGeometry
open Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.RegularIdealSheaf

universe u

namespace Stacks.Intersection.Diagonal

/-- **For a closed immersion, regularity as an immersion is regularity of the defining ideal
sheaf on the target.** An immersion is regular when the defining ideal sheaf of its canonical
closed factor, which lives on the open subscheme `i.coborderRange`, is a regular ideal sheaf;
when `i` is already a closed immersion that open is the whole target and the condition may be
read there. This is the converse of
`Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.isRegularImmersion_of_isRegularIdealSheaf`. -/
theorem isRegularIdealSheaf_of_isRegularImmersion {Z X : Scheme.{u}} (i : Z ⟶ X)
    [IsClosedImmersion i] (h : IsRegularImmersion i) :
    IsRegularIdealSheaf X.toRingedSpace (definingIdealOfHom i) := by
  have hfac : i.liftCoborder ≫ i.coborderRange.ι = i := i.liftCoborder_ι
  have hci : IsClosedImmersion (i.liftCoborder ≫ i.coborderRange.ι) := by
    rw [hfac]; infer_instance
  rw [← hfac]
  exact (Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.isRegularIdealSheaf_comp_open_iff i.liftCoborder
    i.coborderRange.ι).mpr h

/-- **A regular closed immersion has every stalk kernel generated by a regular sequence.** For a
closed immersion `i : Z ⟶ X` which is a regular immersion and a point `z` of `Z`, the kernel of
the induced map of local rings `𝒪_{X, i z} ⟶ 𝒪_{Z, z}` is generated by a regular sequence of
`𝒪_{X, i z}`. No condition on the point is needed: every point of the image lies in the support
of the quotient by the defining ideal sheaf. -/
theorem exists_isRegular_generating_ker_stalkMap_of_isRegularImmersion {Z X : Scheme.{u}}
    (i : Z ⟶ X) [IsClosedImmersion i] (h : IsRegularImmersion i) (z : Z) :
    ∃ rs : List (X.presheaf.stalk (i z)),
      RingTheory.Sequence.IsRegular (X.presheaf.stalk (i z)) rs ∧
        Ideal.ofList rs = RingHom.ker (i.stalkMap z).hom :=
  exists_isRegular_generating_ker_stalkMap i (isRegularIdealSheaf_of_isRegularImmersion i h) z
    (mem_idealQuotientSupport_definingIdealOfHom i z)

/-- **The ideal of the diagonal of a nonsingular variety is generated by a regular sequence at
every point.** Let `X` be a nonsingular variety over a perfect field `k` and `ξ` a point of `X`.
Then the kernel of the map of local rings `𝒪_{X ×ₖ X, Δ(ξ)} ⟶ 𝒪_{X, ξ}` induced by the diagonal
is generated by a regular sequence of `𝒪_{X ×ₖ X, Δ(ξ)}`.

The perfect-field hypothesis is the one Stacks tag 0B0Q carries, and it is not an obstruction
for the reduction to the diagonal: the chapter on intersection theory fixes an algebraically
closed ground field, which is perfect, so the hypothesis is discharged by the chapter's own
convention. The length of the sequence is left as it comes; its identification with `dim X` is
not asserted. -/
theorem exists_isRegular_generating_ker_stalkMap_diagonal {k : Type u} [Field k]
    [PerfectField k] {X : Scheme.{u}} (f : X ⟶ Spec (.of k)) [IsVariety k f]
    (hX : IsRegular X) (ξ : X) :
    ∃ rs : List ((pullback f f).presheaf.stalk (pullback.diagonal f ξ)),
      RingTheory.Sequence.IsRegular
        ((pullback f f).presheaf.stalk (pullback.diagonal f ξ)) rs ∧
        Ideal.ofList rs = RingHom.ker ((pullback.diagonal f).stalkMap ξ).hom :=
  exists_isRegular_generating_ker_stalkMap_of_isRegularImmersion (pullback.diagonal f)
    (Stacks.Intersection.isRegularImmersion_diagonal f hX) ξ

end Stacks.Intersection.Diagonal

end
